cartridge "FLIPPER ALLEY/T.BRANTNER/1987"

' FLIPPER ALLEY
' A ColecoVision game based on "Pinball Arcade" by Tony Brantner, Ahoy!
' March 1987 (Commodore 64 machine language, PINBALL ML at $C000, started
' by a two-line BASIC loader). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/pinball-ml.dis); the table is redrawn for the
' ColecoVision: red rails on blue felt, round bumpers that light up, yellow
' studs, two swinging flippers, a silver ball, a spring plunger, bells.
'
' The game: pull the plunger (stick down/up sets the power), launch with a
' button. The ball flies up the lane, past the one-way gate and onto the
' table, bouncing off rails, studs and bumpers. Press a button to flip.
' Bumpers score 100 (cyan, top), 50 (magenta) and 10 (green, low). Every
' 1000 points is an extra ball (at most 9). A ball through the drain
' between the flippers is lost; three balls to start.
'
' From the original (orig/): the ball moves one pixel at a time, X and Y
' separately; each axis has a speed index 0-7 (0-3 up/left from fast to
' slow, 4-7 down/right from slow to fast) that sets how often it moves
' (every 1, 2, 4 or 8 passes, worked out here in pixels a frame, MagT);
' gravity adds 3/256 of a step to the down speed every pass (GravStep);
' a ball that meets a rail turns round on that axis by the table
' 6,5,4,4,3,3,2,1 (BncT: a fast ball comes back a little slower) and a
' bounce up or down also starts the gravity count at random; anything on
' the table that is not felt is solid (rails, studs, bumpers, the gate);
' bumpers score 50, 100, 50, 10, 10 and flash; the thousands digit
' changing gives an extra ball, up to 9; the plunger power 3-15 starts at
' 5, the stick moves it, a button launches; the ball leaves the lane going
' up fast and to the left, faster the harder it was pulled
' ((power / 4) xor 3); a flipped flipper sends the ball up fast (speed 1),
' faster with the stick up (0), softer with the stick down (2), and
' sideways by the table 0,0,1,2,5,6,7,7 (KickT); the score and the high
' score, six digits each.
'
' Liberties: the 40 x 23 table is scaled to 28 x 21 cells (bumpers 2 x 2
' instead of 3 x 3, the eleven 2 x 2 studs become 1 x 1 studs, the two
' over the drain kept side by side as a short bar; the two right-hand
' rows the C64 had at columns 35-37, rows 12-13, are left out: scaled down
' they made a pocket on the stud beside the lane that trapped the ball);
' the passes are turned into whole frames (the C64 ran about 7.5 passes a
' frame, from the cycle count of its delay loop: the speeds here are about
' 0.7 of that, scaled to the smaller table; gravity 17/256 a frame, a bit
' floatier than the C64's, so a flip with the stick up reaches the top
' bumpers); the ball tests two corners of its leading edge (the C64 one
' point). The original's flipper row was solid even at rest and the ball
' bounced off it almost as off a rail, so the game played itself and the
' button only added points: here a resting flipper is dead rubber, the
' ball hops down its slope towards the drain (which, as on the C64, the
' flippers never cover), so the flips must be timed; a flip lasts 10
' frames (kicking during the first 6) with 6 more before the next one
' (the C64's flip lasted 64 passes, about 8 frames), so tapping at random
' keeps few balls; a sound for every kind of bumper, the flip, the launch,
' the drain and an extra ball (the C64 had a bumper buzz and a launch
' click); bumpers stay lit 12 frames (the C64: 21 passes); the score's
' six BCD digits wrap past 999999; GAME OVER then the title (the C64 asked
' PRESS RETURN).
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2). Before the launch: stick down/up sets the
' plunger, a button launches. On the table: either button flips both
' flippers; hold the stick up for a harder flip, down for a softer one.
' On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

